<p>An insurance estimate review in Corpus Christi starts with a simple question: does the estimate describe the damage to your property and what it actually costs to repair it here? Insurer estimates are long, coded, and dense, and the summary page hides the details that matter. A missing roof slope, a low waste factor, or a depreciation rate that is too high can each move the payment by a meaningful amount. Corpus Christi Public Adjuster reviews estimates line by line, compares them with the property and the policy, and explains in plain English what we find.</p>

<h2>What an Estimate Review Looks At</h2>
<p>Most property insurers write estimates in Xactimate. We use the same software, which makes direct, item-by-item comparisons possible. A thorough review checks:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What we check</th><th>Common problems</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Every room, roof slope, elevation, and structure that was damaged</td><td>Missing rooms, slopes, garages, fences, or contents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quantities</td><td>Measurements, square footage, roof squares, and waste factors</td><td>Undercounted areas, low waste, missing ridge or hip lengths</td></tr>
<tr><td>Method</td><td>Repair versus replace decisions for each item</td><td>Spot repairs where materials cannot be repaired or matched</td></tr>
<tr><td>Components</td><td>Underlayment, flashing, drip edge, vents, trim, and code items</td><td>Accessories and code-required items left out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Unit prices against the local market and the date of loss</td><td>Pricing that lags after a major storm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Rates, ages, and what is recoverable under the policy</td><td>Excessive depreciation or wrong age assumptions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deductibles and payments</td><td>Which deductible applies and how prior payments are credited</td><td>Wrong deductible type, double-counted payments</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other coverages</td><td>Debris removal, additional living expense, business income, ordinance or law</td><td>Coverages never addressed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

