<h2>How the Review Works</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Collect the documents.</strong> We start with the full estimate, payment letters, the declarations page, and your photos.</li>
<li><strong>Read the policy.</strong> We note replacement cost or actual cash value terms, deductibles, exclusions, and endorsements that affect the estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect the property.</strong> We document the damage with photos, measurements, drone images where useful, and Matterport 3D scans on larger losses.</li>
<li><strong>Compare line by line.</strong> We mark each missing, short, or mispriced item and note why.</li>
<li><strong>Explain the findings.</strong> You get a plain-English summary of the differences and what may be worth pursuing.</li>
<li><strong>Decide next steps together.</strong> That may be a supplement, a reinspection, appraisal, or accepting the estimate if it is reasonable.</li>
</ol>
<p>Sometimes the review shows that the insurer's estimate is close to the actual cost. When that is the case, we will say so.</p>

<h2>Reviewing Engineer and Inspector Reports</h2>
<p>On larger or disputed claims, the insurer may rely on a report from an engineer, a roof inspector, or another consultant. These reports often drive the estimate: if the report concludes that only one slope shows storm damage, the estimate will usually reflect one slope. That makes the report as important as the estimate itself.</p>
<p>When we review a consultant report, we look at what the inspector actually examined, how many test squares or sample areas were checked, what weather data was considered for the date of loss, and whether the photos support the conclusions. NOAA storm records, including the Storm Events Database, can show reported hail and wind near the property on the date in question. We also compare the report's observations with what we document on our own inspection. When the findings differ, we explain the differences in writing and with photos, so the insurer has a specific basis to reconsider. Ask your insurer in writing for a copy of any report it used, so you can see the basis for its position.</p>

<h2>Reading Your Estimate Yourself</h2>
<p>If you want to look before calling anyone, a few checks go a long way:</p>
<ul>
<li>Compare the rooms and roof slopes listed in the estimate with the damage you see.</li>
<li>Look for the roof's total squares and the waste percentage, and compare them with a roofer's measurement.</li>
<li>Check whether depreciation is marked as recoverable and note the deadline in your policy to claim it.</li>
<li>Confirm the deductible amount and type on the estimate against your declarations page.</li>
<li>Look for items like debris removal, permits, and contents.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a> walks through a roof estimate in detail, and the <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how depreciation and deductibles change the net payment.</p>

<h2>What Happens After the Review</h2>
<p>When the review finds missing or underpriced items, the usual next step is a supplement with line items, photos, and explanations. See <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a>. If the insurer reviews the supplement and the disagreement is still about amount rather than coverage, the policy's appraisal clause, or TWIA's statutory appraisal process for windstorm claims, may be an option. Our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page explains both. If the estimate is low because part of the claim was denied, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and our denied claims resources.</p>
<p>Every Corpus Christi Public Adjuster engagement starts with an honest conversation about what the review shows. We cannot promise a higher payment, and results depend on the policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> also publishes consumer information on property claims. To have your estimate reviewed, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
